00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Now, as mentioned, we are going to be
looking in this video at peg ratio.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:41.000
And unfortunately, or fortunately,
depending on which way you look at it,
it's unfortunate for people who think this
way, but fortunate for those that don't.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:51.000
A peg ratio is as misinterpreted as PE
ratio by many individuals in the market.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:06.000
A peg ratio is simply a valuation metric
that's built on the standard PE ratio, and
it's calculated by dividing forward PE by
forward earnings growth.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:13.000
And we take the current year, which is
still forward looking and the following
year.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.000
So it's worked out firstly as PE.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:28.000
So the stock price live in the market
right now divided by forward earnings per
share and then divided by forward earnings
per share percentage growth.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:33.000
So forward EG forward earnings growth.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:47.000
Now in conventional or traditional
academic finance theory, we've already
seen this in previous videos when we
talked about price earnings ratio.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:07:02.000
In traditional academic finance theory,
they will tell you high PE translates into
the word expensive, which is implying that
a stock or a company is overvalued.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.000
And what would be the implied action on
that?

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:10.000
It would be to sell a long position or go
short.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:23.000
In traditional academic finance theory, a
low PE translates into the word cheap,
which implies a company or a stock is
undervalued.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.000
And what does that translate into?

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.000
What action does it translate into?

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:37.000
It implies to initiate a long position or
cover a short position, meaning you buy
the stock.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:47.000
Similarly, because PEG is worked out as PE
over the EPS growth rate over EG, the
earnings growth percentage.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:08:00.000
So similarly with PEG ratio, a high PEG
and a low PEG supposedly translates into
expensive and cheap respectively.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.000
Nonsense.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:06.000
Tear up your university finance theory
textbooks.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.000
It's complete bullshit.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:12.000
Cheap and expensive means nothing for
traders.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.000
Something can stay cheap forever.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:13.000
And something can stay expensive forever.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:13.000
And something can stay expensive forever.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:13.000
And something can stay expensive forever.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:13.000
From the context of a PEG ratio.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.000
A PEG ratio.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.000
What does it represent?

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:15.000
It means expensive.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:15.000
It means expensive.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.000
It means expensive.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:16.000
It means expensive.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.000
It means expensive.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.000
It means expensive.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.000
It means expensive.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:19.000
It means expensive.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.000
It means expensive.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.000
It means expensive.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.000
It means expensive.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:22.000
It means expensive.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.000
It means expensive.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:23.000
It means expensive.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.000
It means expensive.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.000
It means expensive.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:26.000
It means expensive.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.000
It means expensive.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:28.000
It means expensive.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.000
It means nothing for traders.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.000
It means expensive.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.000
It means nothing for traders.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:33.000
It means expensive.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.000
It means expensive.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.000
And something can stay expensive forever.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:40.000
From the context of a PEG ratio.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.000
Well, a PEG ratio.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.000
What does it represent?

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:53.000
It represents the price the market is
willing to pay for a company's forecasted
earnings per share percentage growth.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.000
Similar to the PE ratio.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.000
There is no such thing as cheap and
expensive.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.000
A PEG ratio can stay the same while a
stock rises and falls.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.000
So, let's have a look at an example here.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.000
Let's have a look at a stock that we've
just called Stock A.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:19.000
The stock price currently live in the
market is $10 per share.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:23.000
The forward EPS is $1 per share.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:27.000
Therefore, the price earnings ratio is 10
times earnings.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.000
A PE ratio of 10.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.000
The EPS growth, forward EPS growth, is
20%.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.000
So, what's the PEG ratio?

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.000
It's 10 divided by 20, which gives us 0.5.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:53.000
Now, we can use the PEG formula to work
out some other very interesting and
important information when we wish to do
so.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:10:03.000
So, for example, if we wanted to work out
last year's reported EPS without even
looking at it.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:16.000
Well, that can be obtained by simply
discounting this year's expected forward
looking EPS by the expected EPS growth
percentage.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:34.000
So, last year's EPS can be worked out by
taking the $1 forward EPS and dividing by
1.2, which is the 20% earnings growth and
we get 83.33 cents.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:41.000
Of course, we would always check the
reported EPS because it's public
information.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:52.000
But this is a good way of reconciling it
and making sure that last year's reported
EPS, we have it accurately in our trade
idea generation process.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:59.000
And also that we have our forward looking
earnings growth correct in our trade idea
generation process.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:17.000
Additionally, if we're sitting in the
market at any one moment in time and a
company announces, let's say, a material
change in business conditions and guides
the market to a lower forward looking EPS.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:22.000
In this example, we've used 90 cents.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:37.000
So, let's say, in this example, the
company that was previously expecting
forward EPS of $1, they cite a material
change in business conditions and they
guide lower to 90 cents.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:46.000
Well, this changes the EPS growth estimate
from 20% to 8%.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.000
So, we know our last year reported EPS
through our two methods.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:12:06.000
Firstly, checking the publicly available
information as it's reported and also
reconciling with our previous forward
looking estimate of $1 of earnings and 20%
earnings growth.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:15.000
We know that last year's EPS was 83.33
cents.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:24.000
If they guide down forward looking EPS to
90 cents, then we can work out the
percentage earnings growth very easily.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:36.000
So, we have to do 90 cents minus 83.33
cents divided by 83.33 cents, which in
this example gives us 8%.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:46.000
What we can also do very usefully is solve
our PEG formula for P.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:55.000
So, we solve for the stock price and what
we can do is we can solve it for the stock
price that keeps the PEG ratio constant.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:59.000
So, we solve the PEG formula for P.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:18.000
Now, if we plug in our numbers to our PEG
formula, our PEG formula is stock price
divided by our new forward looking EPS
number divided by our new forward looking
earnings growth percentage number.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:27.000
So, we want to solve for the PEG and we
want to solve for the PEG ratio of 0.5.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:46.000
We've rearranged the formula to make the P
and we calculate it as 0.5 multiplied by
8% multiplied by 90 cents, which gives a
stock price of $3.60 if we maintain the
same PEG ratio level of 0.5.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:14:02.000
And if you recall, our original stock
price in this example was $10.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:05.000
So, in this example, what has happened
here?

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:18.000
Well, the company has downgraded guidance
and guided the market lower from a forward
looking EPS number of $1 per share.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:24.000
And they've downgraded to a guidance of 90
cents per share.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.000
So, 90 cents EPS.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:45.000
And what we can very easily work out from
that move in EPS is work out if the market
paid the same level of PEG ratio in the
new dynamic versus the old dynamic.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:55.000
If we make that assumption, where will the
stock trade if it's valued by the market
under the same assumption?

00:14:55.000 --> 00:15:06.000
So, in this example, a 10-cent move down
from $1 to 90 cents in forward looking
EPS.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:19.000
By keeping PEG ratio constant, that
10-cent move has resulted in a fall in the
stock price of $6.40.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:26.000
So, from $10, that's a 64% drop to $3.60.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:32.000
Now, as traders and PMs, this is just
useful information to know for a couple of
reasons.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:44.000
Firstly, we can look at the sensitivity of
valuation and stock price to a change in
EPS.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:49.000
And don't forget, this could also happen
in the positive as well.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:57.000
So, a company could cite a material change
in business conditions to the upside, not
just the downside.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:08.000
So, we could use this formula to work out
upside as well as downside.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:23.000
So, what we're doing here as traders and
portfolio managers is essentially working
out what the potential downside and upside
could be based on similar valuation
metrics in new dynamics.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:36.000
Additionally, it also shows very obviously
that a stock can go down as much as it
wants and still be on the same PEG ratio.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:39.000
And the opposite holds true.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:49.000
A stock can go up to a level of whatever
it wants and the PEG ratio can still be on
the same level.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:17:02.000
So, always, always, always, always
remember, a stock can go from any price to
its lowest price possible and still
maintain the same PEG ratio.

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:14.000
And a stock can go from its lowest price
possible to any price possible and still
maintain the same PEG ratio.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:20.000
Cheap and expensive are curse words in
professional trading circles.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:25.000
Nothing is ever cheap or expensive for
traders.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:26.000
Okay?

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:36.000
Both price-earnings ratio and PEG ratio
are a signal of the quality of the
earnings of a company or stock.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:43.000
Always think to yourself the following
when you're going through your trade idea
generation process.

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:45.000
We mentioned this in previous videos.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:53.000
What price is the market willing to pay
for or willing to sell the earnings of the
company at?

00:17:54.000 --> 00:18:01.000
There must be a reason why a positive
outlier trades on a premium to the sector.

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:07.000
And a reason why this negative outlier
trades on a discount to the sector.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:15.000
And of course, always recall, there's
nothing you know that the market doesn't
already know.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:22.000
And the market can always stay irrational
longer than you can stay solvent.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:25.000
Don't argue with the market.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:40.000
Now, when it comes to PEG ratio, as you're
going through your processes, you are
going to treat PEG ratio in the same way
as you treat PE ratio and earnings growth.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:47.000
You're going to look at PEG ratio on a
relative basis within a sector.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:52.000
So you look at a company's PEG ratio
compared to other stocks.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:19:00.000
And here, we're going to be looking again
at Shopify, Wayfair and Overstock.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:08.000
And in the table that should be familiar
to you by now, we have filtered here for
PEG 1.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:12.000
So if you look at the chart, you can see
the column for PEG 1.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:28.000
The PEG 1 for Shopify, if you look in the
column, is a total mess because of the
negative 0.22% number, which is in the EG
1 column.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:38.000
So remember, the PEG ratio is the PE
divided by the earnings growth number.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:49.000
And because of the laws of small numbers
here, the PE of 287 is divided by the
0.22% negative number.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:56.000
So we get a crazy PEG 1 for Shopify.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:05.000
The number could be very, very different
by tiny marginal changes in this 0.22
negative number.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:13.000
It could be divided by 0, it could be
divided by 1, 2 or 3%, or even negative 1,
2 or 3%.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:17.000
And we would get very, very different
numbers.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:21.000
And this is going to happen to you a lot
when you go through your processing.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:26.000
You are going to see some wild numbers
because of the law of small numbers.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:28.000
Don't worry about that for the moment.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:33.000
We are going to come on to this later in
the trade idea generation process.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:42.000
Now, from our previous work on valuation
and on this sector, we learnt quite
quickly that in this sector,

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:48.000
the market is looking beyond current year
earnings.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:50.000
So looking beyond year one.

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:55.000
And focusing also very heavily on revenue
growth.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:21:03.000
Additionally, when we look at PEG ratios
for Overstock and Wayfair,

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:13.000
we learn some important information in
that we know Overstock has a higher
earnings growth 2 number than earnings
growth 1.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:19.000
And if we have higher earnings growth
percentage in EG2 versus EG1,

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:26.000
then PEG 2 will be lower than PEG 1 as it
stands at that moment in time.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:32.000
Why? Because we're dividing PE by a bigger
number in period 2.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:37.000
Therefore, the PEG is going to be lower
than in PEG 1.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:48.000
And you can see this very clearly in the
Overstock example where we have earnings
growth 1 of 7.26%

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:53.000
and earnings growth 2 of 64.66%.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:22:00.000
We divide the PE by those numbers and we
get the PEG 1 and the PEG 2 respectively.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:07.000
We get a PEG 1 of 9.23 and a PEG 2 of
0.57.

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:19.000
So what we're seeing here with Overstock,
not as extreme in terms of a move with the
law of small numbers as Shopify,

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:30.000
but the law of small numbers is applying
here in the first period because we have a
low earnings growth number of 7.26%.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:40.000
So if we divide that 67 PE by 7.26%
earnings growth, we just get a high number
9.23.

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:50.000
A small change in that, perhaps if we
divide it by say 12, we'd see the PEG come
down by quite a lot.

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:54.000
But that earnings growth would be a very
small difference in EPS.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:23:01.000
So if we divide it by 12, for example,
instead of 7.26, it would come out at
around 5.5%.

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:16.000
And because the earnings growth is jumping
by quite a lot from 7% to around 64, 65%,
we have a much lower PEG ratio for PEG 2
versus PEG 1.

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:31.000
Now, in the example of Wayfair, for
earnings growth 1 and 2, we do have, in
period 2, a much higher level of earnings
growth than in EG 1.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:41.000
So EG 2 comes in at just under 152%
earnings growth, but we're coming from a
negative position.

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:51.000
So the PEG 1 is going to be a negative
PEG, and the PEG 2 is going to be a
positive PEG.

00:23:51.000 --> 00:24:00.000
So the PEG 2 is not going to be lower in
this situation because we're coming from a
negative level.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:06.000
So in situations like this, when you're
going through your trade idea generation
process,

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:21.000
it becomes pretty obvious that from a
trading and portfolio management
perspective, we can't really read too much
into the importance of the PEG 1 numbers
for this sector.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:34.000
What is more important here is the PEG 2
numbers, not simply because the numbers
are all skewed and messed up in period 1
for PEG 1,

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:48.000
but also because we know from our previous
work, what we know about this sector is
that the market is looking past current
year earnings and looking towards next
year's earnings,

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:53.000
and also focusing a lot on revenue growth
as well.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:25:01.000
So in a situation that's typical to this
outcome, we have to filter for PEG 2.

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:10.000
And when we look at Shopify, the PEG 2 now
comes out at 9.45.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:14.000
This is obviously a very high PEG.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:18.000
And we obviously read into it as a
positive sign.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:24.000
But of course, 9.45 for a PEG ratio is
pretty insane.

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:35.000
We'll come on to this in a moment about
sensible levels of assumptions for PEG
ratios from a trading and portfolio
management perspective.

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:37.000
But we do take it as positive.

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:45.000
We take it as a signal of the quality of
the revenue and future earnings of
Shopify.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:49.000
That's what the market seems to be paying
for.

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:58.000
As traders, we acknowledge it and allow
long-term investors to have their fun in
the stock and hold the baby.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:02.000
Non-participation for us is an option.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:12.000
If you remember, we eliminated shop as a
potential trade idea due to the size of
its market cap.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:15.000
So as traders, we don't argue with the
market.

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:17.000
We just step back.

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:20.000
We choose to not participate.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:30.000
And we allow long-term investors to invest
in Shopify at the valuation that they
believe is justified at this moment in
time.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:33.000
And we allow them to hold the baby.

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:36.000
For us, non-participation is an option.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:52.000
And for the record, later in the trade
idea generation process, we will further
explain why very high market cap stocks
are eliminated from a traders process.

00:26:52.000 --> 00:27:08.000
Now, if we look at Wayfair and Overstock
on a PEG-2 basis, Wayfair has a PEG-2 of
0.42 and Overstock has a PEG-2 of 0.57.

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:20.000
Now, we initially, firstly, interpret this
in a negative manner for Overstock, for
example, because we ask ourselves the
question, why?

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:22.000
What's the reason?

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:23.000
There must be a reason.

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:30.000
Is the market willing to sell the quality
of the 2022 earnings growth?

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:36.000
However, we also acknowledge that the
stock is trading now and trades every day.

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:44.000
So there may be an expectation of period
two already in period one and in the stock
price.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:56.000
So we acknowledge that there is a conflict
here in the numbers in that Overstock's
PEG-1 is 9.23.

00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:03.000
So in a situation like this, we can revert
to our Solve4P formula.

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:12.000
So we can work out for the PEG to remain
the same in 2022 versus 2021.

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:15.000
What does the stock price cut out us?

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:28.000
Well, when we Solve4P, we're multiplying
9.23 by the 64.66% EG-2 earnings growth
number.

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:33.000
And we multiply that by $2.19 EPS.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:43.000
And we get to a stock price of $1,307 with
a current share price in the market of
Overstock of $80.

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:52.000
Now, this is obviously ridiculous because
the 9.23 number is very heavily skewed in
a PEG-1

00:28:52.000 --> 00:29:01.000
because of the law of small numbers in the
EG-1 number for Overstock at 7.26% that we
mentioned previously.

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:04.000
But we can make some assumptions here.

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:20.000
We can apply a normal healthy PEG ratio,
which in the financial markets is between
1 and 2 for a company that trades on a
premium to its earnings growth.

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:25.000
So its PE is trading on a higher number
than its earnings growth.

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:36.000
So a normal and healthy PEG ratio that
signifies a market endorsement
traditionally is around 1 to 2.

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:48.000
In the Overstock example, the market here
must be paying a premium now for something
else in the future.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:30:05.000
And if we recall from previous videos, the
revenue growth of Overstock in 2022,
period 2, was 15.62%.

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:11.000
That was the forecasted number for
Overstock revenue growth in 2022.

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:21.000
And that number was higher than in all
previous years to the COVID-19 economic
lockdown year in 2020.

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:36.000
So the market is possibly looking through
year one and looking to year two, 2022,
and looking at revenue growth and giving
benefit to the stock price now.

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:42.000
Now, and this is reflective in the PEG one
number.

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:53.000
And obviously reflected in the one year
forward current year PE ratio, which if
you recall is 67.

00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:59.000
And that's divided by a low earnings
growth number in period one.

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:12.000
So to get that PEG ratio in year one of
just above nine, you not only need a low
earnings growth number, but you also need
a high PE ratio.

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:37.000
So it's possible here that in year one,
the current stock price in Overstock and
the PE ratio is creating a high PEG ratio
because of the low earnings growth,
because the market is looking through the
current year and looking at the revenue
growth in period two.

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:54.000
And of course, not only revenue growth is
important here for the market in 2022, but
also earnings growth in period two is
obviously very important.

00:31:54.000 --> 00:32:02.000
So we know that for overstock, we know
that for overstock, the earnings growth in
period two is 64.66%.

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:26.000
So if we make the assumption that PE ratio
and PE ratio in 2021 in period one is
somewhat reflective of what is occurring
in period two with revenue growth of
15.62% and earnings growth of 64.66%.

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:44.000
We can make an assumption here of the
market potentially rewarding overstock if
they deliver on these numbers in period
two by putting the stock on a PEG ratio of
one.

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:51.000
And then we can solve for P, we can solve
for stock price.

00:32:51.000 --> 00:33:12.000
And when we do this, it's quite
interesting because when we solve for P on
a PEG ratio of one using the EG2 64.66%
and the EPS in period two, we get to a
stock price of $140.60.

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:28.000
So if the market rewards overstock on a
PEG ratio of one in period two, the stock
price has potential upside here to
$140.00.

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:37.000
And this is from a current stock price at
the time of recording for overstock of
$80.00.

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:47.000
So this would represent a 75% increase
approximately in the share price over a
one-year period.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:58.000
Now what else is interesting is if you
look at the difference in the PE ratios
between period one and period two for
overstock.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:14.000
In period one, we had a PE ratio of 67%
and in period two, we had a PE ratio of
36.67% and the difference between the PE
ratios is around 80%.

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:42.000
So moving forward from period one to
period two, if the stock price becomes
reflective of fundamentals in period two
and overstock trades on the same PE
multiple in period two versus period one,
which is 67% and we make a reasonable
assumption that the market rewards
overstock.

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:46.000
If the stock price rewards overstock for
hitting its numbers, then we get to a
share price of roughly $140.00.

00:34:46.000 --> 00:35:08.000
This outcome combined with the EPS kicker
that overstock gets from paring down its
R&D spend, which we went over in the
previous videos, provides us as traders
with upside potential.

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:13.000
And we've gone through our process here
and recognize this.

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:18.000
At this moment in time, it's an
identification process.

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:24.000
We have identified a positive outlier that
now has potential upside.

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:29.000
And the market, it's clear, possibly
thinks this too.

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:33.000
The question really is whether it's
probable.

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:57.000
So the future stock price outcome of
overstock is probably going to be
determined by the management's ability to
stay on track and keep reiterating the
15.62% revenue growth or even better than
that for 2022.

00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:12.000
And maintain profitability as a company
coming from a loss-making position in
previous years, whilst paring down R&D
spent.

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:20.000
On current valuations, it seems the market
is expecting them to be able to do this to
an extent.

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:25.000
But they're not giving the full benefit to
the company yet.

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:27.000
They need to deliver.

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:44.000
So on current valuations, the PE is
reflective, the high PE of 67 now is
reflective of an expectation from the
market that they're going to be able to
deliver to an extent.

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:45.000
We say to an extent.

00:36:45.000 --> 00:37:07.000
We say to an extent because when you
combine this with a PEG ratio of 0.5
that's lower than a PEG ratio of 1, it
means that the market is not giving them
the full benefit of delivering on this
revenue and earnings growth in period two.

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:29.000
If they deliver, then the stock will
probably trade on similar multiples from a
PE perspective in period two versus period
one and will probably trade on a PEG ratio
that's one or higher.

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:40.000
And of course, no one said that in
financial markets and especially in
trading, there's no such thing as risk.

00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:46.000
If they don't deliver on this, the market,
the stock will get killed.

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:49.000
The market will punish the stock.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:57.000
So they need to deliver on the 15.62% of
revenue growth or better for 2022.

00:37:57.000 --> 00:38:06.000
And they need to make sure the needle of
profitability is moving in the right
direction whilst paring down R&D spend.

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:11.000
If they do that, the market will probably
reward them.

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:15.000
If they don't do it, the markets will
probably punish them.

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:24.000
So if they are rewarded, they will trade
on the same or higher multiple in period
two versus period one.

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:28.000
And the stock will be essentially
re-rated.

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:38.000
If they don't deliver, the stock will get
punished and trade on a lower multiple in
period two versus period one.

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:45.000
And the stock will be re-rated to the
downside or de-rated.

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:56.000
Now, as mentioned many times throughout,
and it's worth mentioning again, the
process here is what is important.

00:38:56.000 --> 00:39:03.000
We will always go through similar
processes in every situation.

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:12.000
We have price earnings ratio, earnings
growth, peg ratio, and market cap measures
relative to peer group.

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:19.000
So we have these measures for a stock
relative to peer group, to a sector.

00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:25.000
And we locate positive and or negative
outliers in a sector.

00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:34.000
And we ask the obvious questions while
sticking to our traders' interpretation of
valuation metrics.

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:36.000
We keep asking and we keep digging.

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:47.000
As we're going through the process, we
interpret what messages the market is
telling us and try to come to logical
conclusions.

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:56.000
And of course, every situation will be
different and will have to be interpreted
on its own merits.

00:39:56.000 --> 00:40:03.000
But don't worry, this comes with
experience and practice.

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:10.000
At ITPM, we've had many people start off
with no clue what to do.

00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:19.000
And within three to six months, they know
how to interpret things properly in pretty
much all situations.

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:26.000
It's down to process, it's down to
practice, it's down to common sense and
logic.

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:36.000
Now we're going to be moving on to having
a look in our first step of the
quantitative filtering process.

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:42.000
We're going to move on to looking at
backwards looking financial measures.

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:55.000
So in our example that we've been through
so far in the last few videos, where we're
looking at Shopify, Wayfair and Overstock.

00:40:55.000 --> 00:41:07.000
And we've been through the identification
process of the positive outliers and
started to understand what the market
likes and dislikes and possible reasons
why.

00:41:07.000 --> 00:41:30.000
So this next step is what you would start
doing if you wanted to take it further and
start learning all of the past information
of, for example, Overstock and start
looking at it in comparison to, say,
Shopify and Wayfair and even other stocks
in the sector.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:49.000
What we're doing here is looking at some
key backwards looking financial metrics to
fill in the bigger picture and fill in
some of the gaps to understand the full
picture of the story in the sector.

00:41:49.000 --> 00:42:05.000
And of course, by going through that
exercise, we will start learning a lot
more about the individual stocks that
we're potentially thinking about looking
at for long and short ideas.

00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:34.000
But this type of analysis does come with
caveats, not only because it's backwards
looking information and we are traders and
we look forwards, but also because of the
way as traders we interpret valuation
metrics, which is different to the way
most people in the market or traditional
finance teaches.

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:43.000
But it is still necessary, even from a
trading and portfolio management
perspective, to not go into the market
blind.

00:42:43.000 --> 00:42:52.000
We still have to go through these
processes to fill in the gaps and fill in
and understand the bigger picture.

00:42:52.000 --> 00:43:11.000
What we're going to do in this part of the
video series is show you how these
financial measures are calculated, how
they are defined and how we interpret them
as traders and portfolio managers and with
the caveats as well.

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:21.000
So when you're going through this part of
the process, you'll understand and
interpret these numbers a lot better than
most people in the market.

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:34.000
Okay, so let's first put backwards looking
quantitative measures into context here in
our trade idea generation process.

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:48.000
So far, we've looked at forward looking
valuation and growth measures in the
quantitative phase of the bottom up
section of our trade idea generation
process.

00:43:48.000 --> 00:44:01.000
And you can see the metrics that we've
looked at here on the screen market
capitalization, earnings growth, revenue
growth, PE ratio and PEG ratio.

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:26.000
From those forward looking valuations,
market capitalization includes price and
is forward looking, price earnings ratio
and PEG ratio, obviously also include
price and are forward looking and earnings
growth and revenue growth are percentage
differences based on forward looking
estimates of EPS and revenue.

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:48.000
In the first part of the bottom up section
of the trade idea generation process, so
our first quantitative phase, there are
however, further measures we can use to
extend our knowledge about a sector and
stocks within a particular sector.

00:44:48.000 --> 00:45:03.000
So these are enterprise value, EBIT, EBIT
and EBITDA, EBIT over EBIT, so enterprise
value over EBIT and EBIT enterprise value
over EBITDA.

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:10.000
But we have to remember as traders and
portfolio managers, we are forward looking
animals.

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:22.000
And these measures are all backwards
looking and are taken from a company's
publicly available annual accounts for the
previous year.

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:25.000
As traders, we want to always be looking
forward.

00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:39.000
However, we can use these backwards
looking measures and compare sector peers
to each other on these metrics to get a
better understanding of the sector and
stocks.

00:45:39.000 --> 00:45:54.000
So let's start here with enterprise value,
EV, what is it? What does it do?

00:45:54.000 --> 00:46:00.000
Well, enterprise value attempts to measure
a company's total value.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:05.000
It's a similar measure to market
capitalization, but with a twist.

00:46:05.000 --> 00:46:13.000
So market cap is the live share price in
the market multiplied by the outstanding
shares in issue.

00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:23.000
And enterprise value is equal to the
market cap plus the total debt of the
company minus the cash that the company
has.

00:46:23.000 --> 00:46:33.000
So what enterprise value does is it
captures the value of a company in theory
if it were to be taken over.

00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:48.000
So, for example, let's say a company has a
market cap of $10 billion, but had $6
billion of total debt and a positive cash
position of $1.7 billion.

00:46:48.000 --> 00:47:03.000
The enterprise value of this entity would
be the market cap of $10 billion plus the
debt of $6 billion minus the $1.7 billion
of cash, which gives us $14.3 billion.

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:19.000
So if an acquirer came in to this company
and wanted to buy the company outright at
the current market cap, it would have to
pay off the debts, but would use the cash
towards paying off the debts.

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:24.000
So they would end up paying $14.3 billion
in total.

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:28.000
So acquiring the equity, the debt, and the
cash.

00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:40.000
Now, there are, from a trader's and
portfolio manager's perspective,
limitations and caveats that do come with
the calculation of enterprise value.

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:49.000
Firstly, there's a big question always
over any company that has debt.

00:47:49.000 --> 00:47:53.000
The question is, how is the debt actually
being used?

00:47:53.000 --> 00:48:02.000
And there is an overlap with our second
consideration here, which is the quality
of operations at the company itself.

00:48:02.000 --> 00:48:11.000
Thirdly, the market knows the debt and
cash position of the company already.

00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:21.000
It's not like people in the market don't
understand from the last set of accounts
what the debt and cash position is of a
company.

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:22.000
The market knows this.

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:26.000
And lastly, it's obviously backwards
looking.

00:48:26.000 --> 00:48:31.000
So firstly, let's deal with how the debt
is being used.

00:48:31.000 --> 00:48:44.000
So if you are looking, for example, at
sector data in a table and comparing, say,
20 stocks market cap and enterprise value
to each other,

00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:53.000
you may go through the process of locating
stocks that have a lot more debt and lower
cash positions than others.

00:48:53.000 --> 00:48:56.000
Now, does this mean it's a bad stock?

00:48:56.000 --> 00:48:57.000
Not necessarily.

00:48:57.000 --> 00:49:05.000
It's important to remember that enterprise
value does not measure how the debt is
being used.

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:14.000
So you have to actually go and do the work
to figure out how the debt is being used
and what the debt is actually financing.

00:49:14.000 --> 00:49:25.000
If, for example, the debt is being used to
fund highly profitable current and future
operations and very efficient operations,

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:32.000
then you're probably going to see this
reflected in the market cap of the stock
already,

00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:36.000
because the market looks forwards, not
backwards.

00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:41.000
How debt is being used by the company is
very important.

00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:48.000
So when you're comparing all of these
stocks, just because a company has high
debt and lower cash levels than others,

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:51.000
it doesn't mean it's a bad stock in the
sector.

00:49:51.000 --> 00:49:56.000
So the quality of operations at the
company really matters.

00:49:56.000 --> 00:50:09.000
Simply looking at the EV of a stock and
comparing it to another stock's EV may not
add any value to our analysis if we're
already using market cap.

00:50:09.000 --> 00:50:18.000
The question is here is, is it really
going to tell us anything new other than a
statement of where it is?

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:27.000
A relative EV appraisal must be done
within the context of the quality of the
operations at the companies.

00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:39.000
So if you haven't analyzed the operations,
you can't deduce whether enterprise value
is a positive or a negative for a stock
relative to other stocks.

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:49.000
So when you're going through your process
of analyzing EV on a relative basis, you
must bear this in mind.

00:50:49.000 --> 00:51:01.000
You have to learn the operations
themselves and there's many measures of
the quality of operations and it's totally
dependent on the sector that you're in.

00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:13.000
Now this comes later in the trade idea
generation process in the PTM video series
with our qualitative analysis and key
performance indicators.

00:51:13.000 --> 00:51:27.000
Now, additionally, with enterprise value,
we have to understand as traders that the
market obviously knows the debt and cash
position of the company already.

00:51:27.000 --> 00:51:42.000
The market really isn't that stupid. Do we
really think that the market doesn't
already know what the debt and cash
position of a company actually is from
their last set of annual accounts?

00:51:42.000 --> 00:51:55.000
When we look at market capitalization,
it's simply the price that the collective
market is willing to pay for the entire
company given all available information.

00:51:55.000 --> 00:52:09.000
So market cap is therefore a function of
every consideration already, including the
debt of the company, the cash position of
the company and the quality of the
operations.

00:52:09.000 --> 00:52:16.000
Now, we as traders are by no means
believers in efficient market hypothesis.

00:52:16.000 --> 00:52:30.000
So the idea that a company stock price is
a function of perfect information, we
would not be traders if this were true or
if we believed that this was true.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:47.000
However, it's pretty obvious that the
collective market can't be dumb enough to
not already know the outstanding debt and
cash position of a company in its last set
of publicly available accounts.

00:52:48.000 --> 00:52:57.000
We have to assume that they see what we
see, they see it and the price the market
pays for the stock.

00:52:57.000 --> 00:53:05.000
So the market cap is a function of this
plus forward expectations of business
operations.

00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:11.000
Hence why, as traders, forward looking
measures are way more important to us.

00:53:12.000 --> 00:53:21.000
Which brings us on to our next point,
which is forward looking versus backwards
static numbers.

00:53:21.000 --> 00:53:28.000
So forwards looking dynamic numbers versus
backwards looking static accounting
numbers.

00:53:28.000 --> 00:53:35.000
So in the EV calculation, the market cap
number is the dynamic number within the
calculation.

00:53:35.000 --> 00:53:40.000
And it's changing every microsecond based
on forward looking expectations.

00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:55.000
The debt and the cash position numbers
within the EV calculation are static to
the point of the company's last annual or
quarterly update.

00:53:55.000 --> 00:54:02.000
A company can take on more debt or pair
down debt plus the cash position could
change.

00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:11.000
In the last set of accounts, for example,
it may state a particular number for debt
or cash.

00:54:11.000 --> 00:54:27.000
But this can be meaningless if the
company, for example, is providing
guidance that they intend to eliminate a
lot of debt and make, say, business
disposals in the next one to two years to
pay off a lot of debt.

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:44.000
So in this situation, for example, this
would now become a forward looking
qualitative measure and will likely get
reflected in the market cap at some point
well before the financial statements are
updated.

00:54:45.000 --> 00:54:53.000
So with these limitations, the obvious
question is, is why as traders would we
even look at enterprise value?

00:54:53.000 --> 00:54:56.000
Well, it's a good question, but it's for
context.

00:54:56.000 --> 00:55:13.000
We want to have a look at the landscape of
a sector and look at the EV numbers, which
include our market cap plus debt plus cash
position of every company relative to each
other, just to understand what the
landscape is.

00:55:13.000 --> 00:55:26.000
Additionally, we can use enterprise value
and our other backwards looking financial
metrics as either supporting or non
supporting evidence.

00:55:26.000 --> 00:55:37.000
So either confirming or non confirming
evidence of our valuation multiples like
price earnings ratio and peg ratio.

00:55:37.000 --> 00:55:56.000
So for example, when we work out
enterprise value across an entire sector
and we're looking at a positive outlier
that has a high PE and a good high peg
ratio forwards looking in our valuations.

00:55:56.000 --> 00:56:15.000
If we then look at market cap and
enterprise value and we see that there's
not much difference between enterprise
value and market cap, it may mean that the
company has low debt levels and fairly
strong cash position.

00:56:15.000 --> 00:56:30.000
If we look at market cap, it may be a
positive outlier, which could be a driver
or supporting evidence of the high PE
multiple and the high peg ratio relative
to other stocks in the sector.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:47.000
However, what happens if we put our tables
together, we work out all of our EV
calculations and then we see a positive
outlier high multiple for PE.

00:56:47.000 --> 00:56:57.000
We see high PE, we see high PE, we see
high PE ratio and then we see a very high
and big difference between EV and market
cap.

00:56:57.000 --> 00:57:06.000
Well, that just means that the company has
got like large debt levels and possibly
low cash levels.

00:57:06.000 --> 00:57:17.000
And this would be non-supporting or
non-confirmation for our high multiple PE
and our high PE ratio.

00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:19.000
So this would be conflicting information.

00:57:19.000 --> 00:57:29.000
And of course, in either case, we always
take it to the next stage and we put it in
the context of the quality of the
operations.

00:57:29.000 --> 00:57:46.000
But in that situation, as traders and
portfolio managers, we still interpret the
high multiple PE ratio and the high PE
ratio in that the market must be paying
for something else at the company.

00:57:46.000 --> 00:57:52.000
And in that situation, it's highly likely
that it's to do with the quality of the
operations.

00:57:52.000 --> 00:58:07.000
The key point to take away here is that
it's part of the process and we follow the
process and it's repeatable for all
sectors throughout time.

00:58:07.000 --> 00:58:22.000
And we go through this part of the process
as the first part of the trade idea
generation bottom up section of the
overall process in order to get context.

00:58:22.000 --> 00:58:39.000
So when we look at valuation, we either
see confirming or non-confirming
information and then we ask further
questions and we continue our further
lines of inquiry.

00:58:39.000 --> 00:58:44.000
By going through these processes, we are
information gathering.

00:58:44.000 --> 00:59:00.000
We are learning more information about the
sector and stocks and understanding the
context of each stock within the sector
relative to its peer group.

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:22.000
The more information we have, the better
understanding we're going to have about a
particular sector and stocks within the
sector as long as we interpret the numbers
correctly and continue logical lines of
inquiry and ask the right and obvious
questions.

00:59:22.000 --> 00:59:28.000
Next up in our backwards looking financial
measures is EBIT and EBITDA.

00:59:28.000 --> 00:59:43.000
Now, the difference between EBIT and
EBITDA is that depreciation and
amortization have been added back to
earnings in EBITDA while they're not added
back to EBIT.

00:59:43.000 --> 00:59:58.000
So the definition of EBITDA is earnings
before interest, taxes, depreciation and
amortization.

00:59:58.000 --> 01:00:06.000
And EBITDA is net earnings plus interest
expense plus tax expense.

01:00:06.000 --> 01:00:19.000
And EBITDA is net earnings plus tax
expense plus depreciation plus
amortization in the financial accounts.

01:00:19.000 --> 01:00:25.000
Now, EBIT is often referred to as
operating income.

01:00:25.000 --> 01:00:34.000
So the company's earnings prior to paying
interest to creditors and taxes to the IRS
on those earnings.

01:00:34.000 --> 01:00:43.000
And with EBITDA, that is operating income
with depreciation and amortization added
back to profits.

01:00:43.000 --> 01:00:55.000
Now, we have to be careful with these
measures and understand that these
measures are contextual to sectors that
we're looking at.

01:00:55.000 --> 01:01:18.000
So EBITDA, for example, since depreciation
isn't captured by EBITDA, it can lead to
profit distortions for companies that have
a sizable amount of fixed assets and
subsequently substantial depreciation
expenses.

01:01:18.000 --> 01:01:26.000
The larger the depreciation expense, the
more it's going to boost the measure of
EBITDA.

01:01:26.000 --> 01:01:35.000
So there are limitations to this and
everything has to be contextual to the
sector that we're looking at.

01:01:35.000 --> 01:01:45.000
Both measures have limitations, but let's
see what it looks like first to understand
the picture a little better.

01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:57.000
So we have on the screen here the
financial statements for 2018 and 2019 for
a random company.

01:01:57.000 --> 01:02:06.000
And if you look on the left hand side,
we're looking at calculating here the
EBIT.

01:02:06.000 --> 01:02:12.000
So EBIT is net earnings plus interest
expense plus tax expense.

01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:19.000
So net earnings for this company are
$39,860,000,000.

01:02:19.000 --> 01:02:24.000
The interest expense is $500,000,000.

01:02:24.000 --> 01:02:28.000
And the taxes are $15,501,000,000.

01:02:28.000 --> 01:02:31.000
So we add all of these together.

01:02:31.000 --> 01:02:45.000
So from the net earnings number, we are
adding back the interest expense and the
taxes to get to $55,861,000,000 EBIT.

01:02:45.000 --> 01:02:50.000
If you look on the right hand side, we
have the same financial statements.

01:02:50.000 --> 01:02:59.000
But this time to get to EBITDA, we are
adding on to EBIT depreciation and
amortization.

01:02:59.000 --> 01:03:07.000
And you can see in the set of accounts on
the right hand side and even on the left
hand side, it's the same set of accounts.

01:03:07.000 --> 01:03:17.000
You can see the line item for depreciation
and amortization for 2019 comes in at
$15,03,000,000,000.

01:03:17.000 --> 01:03:30.000
So we add this onto our EBIT calculation
to get EBITDA of $70,864,000,000.

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:38.000
And if you haven't noticed yet, this is
obviously a very big company in 2021 in
the United States.

01:03:38.000 --> 01:03:43.000
Very big earnings number, EBIT and EBITDA
number.

01:03:43.000 --> 01:03:49.000
This will be a top company in the S&P 500,
something like an Apple.

01:03:50.000 --> 01:03:59.000
So now that you've seen how it's
calculated, it's a lot easier to
understand its usefulness and limitations.

01:03:59.000 --> 01:04:05.000
So the usefulness and limitations of both
EBIT and EBITDA calculations.

01:04:05.000 --> 01:04:17.000
Now, the most obvious limitation is that a
company can be making money operationally,
but losing money on a net basis.

01:04:17.000 --> 01:04:27.000
At the end of the day, guys, interest
expenses, taxes, depreciation and
amortization are real things.

01:04:27.000 --> 01:04:39.000
And if a company can't make money on a net
basis, who really cares if they are
operationally profitable?

01:04:39.000 --> 01:04:45.000
So we have to be really careful here when
analyzing EBIT and EBITDA.

01:04:45.000 --> 01:05:01.000
And of course, when we analyze any stock
in a sector and a stock relative to other
stocks in the sector, there's obviously
many things in the financial statements to
consider.

01:05:01.000 --> 01:05:09.000
So like, for example, in our Shopify and
Overstock example with their R&D spend.

01:05:09.000 --> 01:05:16.000
R&D spend is obviously an important factor
for these stocks and that sector.

01:05:16.000 --> 01:05:22.000
And we could even add R&D spend back to
earnings too.

01:05:22.000 --> 01:05:28.000
We can theoretically keep adding
everything back till we get back to just
revenue.

01:05:28.000 --> 01:05:32.000
But that wouldn't be a useful exercise for
us.

01:05:32.000 --> 01:05:42.000
The usefulness of EBIT and EBITDA measures
tends to be highly dependent on which
sectors we are looking at.

01:05:42.000 --> 01:05:59.000
So, for example, EBITDA is a more useful
measure of operational performance in the
oil and gas sector, mining sector,
manufacturing and infrastructure sectors
than, for example, the service sector.

01:05:59.000 --> 01:06:03.000
So highly capital intensive industries.

01:06:03.000 --> 01:06:04.000
Why?

01:06:04.000 --> 01:06:11.000
Because they have very large DNA numbers,
very large depreciation and amortization
numbers.

01:06:12.000 --> 01:06:19.000
So when those get added back in, the
EBITDA number flatters the operational
performance of the company.

01:06:20.000 --> 01:06:28.000
So EBIT is a much better measure or more
useful measure for operational performance
in those sectors.

01:06:28.000 --> 01:06:41.000
EBITDA is a more useful measure or tends
to be a more useful measure than EBIT in
industries with a low capital expenditure.

01:06:41.000 --> 01:06:43.000
So low CAPEX.

01:06:44.000 --> 01:06:49.000
The DNA numbers are very small in these
sectors, even non-existent.

01:06:49.000 --> 01:06:53.000
So here it's a good measure for cash flow.

01:06:53.000 --> 01:07:10.000
So with EBIT and EBITDA, we can compare
companies in the same sector to figure out
which ones have struggled in the past
operationally versus those that have
excelled operationally.

01:07:10.000 --> 01:07:20.000
And we can then identify the reasons why
later in our trade idea generation
process.

01:07:20.000 --> 01:07:27.000
So we look at the qualitative elements
that drive the operational performance
later.

01:07:27.000 --> 01:07:40.000
And then we put this into context as
supporting or non-supporting evidence of
our valuation multiples, price earnings
ratio and PEG ratio.

01:07:40.000 --> 01:07:54.000
Now let's have a look at our backwards
looking financial ratios for valuation, EV
over EBIT and EV over EBITDA.

01:07:55.000 --> 01:08:01.000
And these calculations are very
straightforward based on calculations that
we've looked at before.

01:08:01.000 --> 01:08:08.000
EV over EBIT is enterprise value over
earnings before interest and taxes.

01:08:08.000 --> 01:08:18.000
And EV over EBITDA is enterprise value
over earnings before interest, taxes,
depreciation and amortization.

01:08:18.000 --> 01:08:32.000
And don't forget, it's important to
remember that enterprise value includes
market capitalization plus total debt
minus cash.

01:08:32.000 --> 01:08:46.000
And yes, we have our EBIT and EBITDA
calculations at the bottom of the screen
there that we just looked at in the
financial statements of the company that
we just looked at.

01:08:46.000 --> 01:08:58.000
So with these valuation metrics, these
ratios, we have to be, again, very careful
as traders and portfolio managers, how we
interpret them.

01:08:58.000 --> 01:09:14.000
In traditional finance theory, the
enterprise value to earnings before
interest and taxes ratio is a metric used
to determine if a stock is undervalued or
overvalued.

01:09:14.000 --> 01:09:18.000
And we put those words in quotations.

01:09:18.000 --> 01:09:23.000
So if a stock is either cheap or expensive
relative to its peer group.

01:09:23.000 --> 01:09:36.000
In relative peer group terms, traditional
finance theory says that a low EV over
EBITDA means a stock is cheap or
undervalued.

01:09:36.000 --> 01:09:42.000
And if it's high means it's expensive or
overvalued.

01:09:42.000 --> 01:09:48.000
But as traders and portfolio managers, we
know there's fallacies to this approach.

01:09:48.000 --> 01:09:50.000
We know it not to be true.

01:09:50.000 --> 01:10:02.000
So as traders and portfolio managers, we
know that there's going to be major flaws
to the traditional finance theory
approach.

01:10:02.000 --> 01:10:06.000
We learned this from our price earnings
analysis.

01:10:06.000 --> 01:10:10.000
Nothing is ever cheap or expensive.

01:10:10.000 --> 01:10:20.000
The PE valuation, if you recall, is the
level at which the market is willing to
pay for or sell the earnings of the
company.

01:10:20.000 --> 01:10:39.000
Similarly, the EV over EBITDA ratio is the
valuation level at which the market is
willing to pay for or sell the earnings of
the company before interest expenses and
tax expenses.

01:10:39.000 --> 01:10:48.000
Also, we know what the limitations are of
enterprise value, which is on the top line
of our ratio.

01:10:48.000 --> 01:10:54.000
So we have our four reservations here, as
in how is the debt being used?

01:10:54.000 --> 01:10:58.000
What are the quality of the operations of
the company?

01:10:58.000 --> 01:11:09.000
The market, the market obviously knows
already what the debt and cash position of
the company is, and it's a backwards
looking measure.

01:11:09.000 --> 01:11:25.000
So if the market knows the debt and cash
position of a company already, do you
really think the market is that stupid
that they don't already know the interest
expense of the debt, tax expenses,

01:11:25.000 --> 01:11:36.000
tax expenses, and even the depreciation
and amortization numbers from the last set
of publicly available financial accounts.

01:11:36.000 --> 01:11:39.000
Of course, the market knows this
information.

01:11:39.000 --> 01:11:52.000
And also, when you think of the EV
calculation of live market capitalization,
so the market cap plus debt minus cash,

01:11:52.000 --> 01:12:07.000
the debt and cash element is backwards
looking from financial accounts, and the
market cap number is the livestock price
multiplied by the amount of shares
outstanding in issue.

01:12:07.000 --> 01:12:16.000
So what is driving the dynamic part of the
calculation of EV is the live market cap.

01:12:16.000 --> 01:12:27.000
So essentially, if we're using backwards
looking numbers at the EV level plus
market cap, so static numbers like debt
and cash,

01:12:27.000 --> 01:12:36.000
backwards looking static numbers plus a
dynamic forward looking number, market
cap, and that makes up EV.

01:12:36.000 --> 01:12:48.000
When we divide this by EBIT or even
EBITDA, it's the market cap that's
potentially driving the EV over EBITDA
ratio.

01:12:48.000 --> 01:13:03.000
Unless, of course, we're using the market
cap number at the time of the financial
accounts, in which case, EV over EBIT is
even worse in terms of usefulness,

01:13:03.000 --> 01:13:07.000
because it's a totally backwards looking
metric.

01:13:07.000 --> 01:13:22.000
So if we are using the dynamic number for
market cap, and we are using the backwards
looking numbers for debt and cash on the
top line to make enterprise value,

01:13:22.000 --> 01:13:37.000
price value, and we divide that by
backwards looking numbers for EBITDA, then
market cap, the dynamic market cap is
driving the EV over EBITDA ratio,

01:13:37.000 --> 01:13:52.000
which means it's very similar to price
earnings ratio, except it's backwards
looking numbers that are being used in
addition to the forward looking number of
market cap,

01:13:52.000 --> 01:13:56.000
which is made up of price and outstanding
shares.

01:13:56.000 --> 01:14:18.000
So, what we are using this for in our
process is, again, for either supporting
or non-supporting, confirming or
non-confirming information of our forward
looking valuation metrics of both PE and
PEG ratio.

01:14:18.000 --> 01:14:34.000
So, there is a very valid argument here
that states that market capitalization
already reflects the numbers included in
the enterprise value and EBIT
calculations,

01:14:34.000 --> 01:14:43.000
because it's reflected in the share price
plus future expectations of the business
operation.

01:14:43.000 --> 01:14:49.000
Why? Because the share price is the market
and the market is forward looking.

01:14:49.000 --> 01:15:04.000
Now, traditional finance theory would have
you believe that enterprise value, EBIT
and EBITDA numbers matter more than price
and or forward earnings,

01:15:04.000 --> 01:15:12.000
because the information for EV, EBIT and
EBITDA is already reported information.

01:15:12.000 --> 01:15:31.000
It's real. It's tangible. And yes, it's
true that reported information like
enterprise value, EBIT and EBITDA numbers
that have and include backwards looking
static information is real and tangible.

01:15:31.000 --> 01:15:42.000
But we are forward looking traders. We are
looking for edge and we trade on
expectation of opportunity.

01:15:42.000 --> 01:15:52.000
The real question for us as traders is
whether enterprise value over EBIT or
enterprise value over EBITDA, for example.

01:15:52.000 --> 01:16:01.000
So, the information adds anything
incremental to our forward PE measures.

01:16:01.000 --> 01:16:07.000
So, our valuation measures of our expected
future in a stock.

01:16:07.000 --> 01:16:18.000
Similarly, the same arguments apply to
enterprise value over EBITDA calculations
as above with EBIT.

01:16:18.000 --> 01:16:37.000
So, we have even more reservations with EV
over EBITDA, we are simply adding back
depreciation and amortization to EBIT to
get a higher profit number.

01:16:37.000 --> 01:16:45.000
So, let's say, for example, we are
operating in a highly capital intensive
industry.

01:16:45.000 --> 01:16:52.000
And we're adding back a lot of DNA expense
for, say, a mining operation.

01:16:52.000 --> 01:17:08.000
Do we really think that the market is so
stupid that they don't know that the
mining business owns and operates mines
with sizable fixed assets and capital
expenditure?

01:17:08.000 --> 01:17:19.000
Can we really value a mining business by
analyzing earnings and completely
forgetting about DNA expense and drawing
sensible conclusions?

01:17:19.000 --> 01:17:27.000
Of course not. The market already knows it
exists and takes it into consideration.

01:17:27.000 --> 01:17:34.000
On the top line of the ratio, we have
enterprise value, which includes debt.

01:17:34.000 --> 01:17:41.000
And on the bottom line, we have EBITDA,
which includes depreciation and
amortization.

01:17:41.000 --> 01:17:53.000
But the debt for the company has probably
been used to buy the equipment that
creates the actual depreciation and
amortization.

01:17:53.000 --> 01:18:06.000
So, again, as EBITDA is a better measure
for highly capital intensive industries
and EBITDA is a better measure for low
capital intensive industries,

01:18:06.000 --> 01:18:15.000
when we're looking at operational
performance, EV over EBIT and EV over
EBITDA apply in the same way,

01:18:15.000 --> 01:18:23.000
but also including the limitations of
enterprise value versus market cap.

01:18:23.000 --> 01:18:34.000
So, always bear this in mind when
processing the quant and measuring stocks
relative to each other and in absolute
terms.

01:18:34.000 --> 01:18:48.000
So, in terms of our trade idea generation
processing, how do we use EV, EBIT,
EBITDA, EV over EBIT and EV over EBITDA?

01:18:48.000 --> 01:18:51.000
What is the usefulness here and what do we
use it for?

01:18:51.000 --> 01:19:07.000
Well, in our trade idea generation
processing, we can, of course, add columns
to our spreadsheets to include EV, EBIT,
EBITDA and EV over EBIT and EV over EBITDA

01:19:07.000 --> 01:19:12.000
and make comparisons across a sector, so
across stocks.

01:19:12.000 --> 01:19:17.000
However, we have to understand the
interpretation properly.

01:19:17.000 --> 01:19:25.000
We've seen the drawbacks of EV and EBIT
and EBITDA, so we have to be very careful
with our interpretation.

01:19:25.000 --> 01:19:37.000
Our starting point in processing is
identifying what the market likes and
dislikes, then moving on to ask the
questions, why?

01:19:37.000 --> 01:19:40.000
Why does the market like a particular
stock?

01:19:40.000 --> 01:19:44.000
Why does the market dislike a particular
stock?

01:19:44.000 --> 01:19:47.000
Why does something trade on a premium?

01:19:47.000 --> 01:19:50.000
Why does something trade on a discount?

01:19:50.000 --> 01:19:52.000
What is the justification for the premium?

01:19:52.000 --> 01:19:55.000
What is the justification for the
discount?

01:19:55.000 --> 01:19:59.000
And we continue with lines of inquiry.

01:19:59.000 --> 01:20:04.000
So, we always start in our table with the
following columns.

01:20:04.000 --> 01:20:06.000
Stock price.

01:20:06.000 --> 01:20:08.000
Market cap.

01:20:08.000 --> 01:20:10.000
EPS last year.

01:20:10.000 --> 01:20:12.000
Last reported EPS.

01:20:12.000 --> 01:20:14.000
So, EPS zero.

01:20:14.000 --> 01:20:18.000
Forward-looking one-year EPS.

01:20:18.000 --> 01:20:19.000
The current year.

01:20:19.000 --> 01:20:20.000
EPS one.

01:20:20.000 --> 01:20:22.000
Next year's EPS.

01:20:22.000 --> 01:20:24.000
EPS two.

01:20:24.000 --> 01:20:25.000
EPS two.

01:20:25.000 --> 01:20:26.000
Earnings growth.

01:20:26.000 --> 01:20:27.000
Forward-looking.

01:20:27.000 --> 01:20:28.000
In the first year.

01:20:28.000 --> 01:20:29.000
The current year.

01:20:29.000 --> 01:20:30.000
EG one.

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:31.000
Earnings growth.

01:20:31.000 --> 01:20:33.000
In the following year.

01:20:33.000 --> 01:20:34.000
EG two.

01:20:34.000 --> 01:20:35.000
Price earnings ratio.

01:20:35.000 --> 01:20:36.000
Forward-looking.

01:20:36.000 --> 01:20:37.000
Current year.

01:20:37.000 --> 01:20:38.000
One.

01:20:38.000 --> 01:20:39.000
PE one.

01:20:39.000 --> 01:20:40.000
PE two.

01:20:40.000 --> 01:20:41.000
Next year.

01:20:41.000 --> 01:20:42.000
For the second year.

01:20:42.000 --> 01:20:43.000
PE one.

01:20:43.000 --> 01:20:44.000
Next year.

01:20:44.000 --> 01:20:45.000
Next year.

01:20:45.000 --> 01:20:46.000
For the second year.

01:20:46.000 --> 01:20:47.000
PEG one.

01:20:47.000 --> 01:20:48.000
For the current year.

01:20:48.000 --> 01:20:50.000
One year forward-looking.

01:20:50.000 --> 01:20:51.000
The current year.

01:20:51.000 --> 01:20:52.000
PEG one.

01:20:52.000 --> 01:20:53.000
And PEG two.

01:20:53.000 --> 01:20:54.000
The PEG ratio.

01:20:54.000 --> 01:20:55.000
For next year.

01:20:55.000 --> 01:20:57.000
And then.

01:20:57.000 --> 01:20:59.000
When we have identified.

01:20:59.000 --> 01:21:00.000
Positive.

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:02.000
And negative outliers.

01:21:02.000 --> 01:21:03.000
We move.

01:21:03.000 --> 01:21:04.000
To the next level.

01:21:04.000 --> 01:21:06.000
Of our quantum processing.

01:21:06.000 --> 01:21:08.000
And we start.

01:21:08.000 --> 01:21:09.000
Looking.

01:21:09.000 --> 01:21:10.000
Looking.

01:21:10.000 --> 01:21:11.000
For the reasons.

01:21:11.000 --> 01:21:12.000
Why.

01:21:12.000 --> 01:21:13.000
The market likes.

